  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why does a seatbelt let me lean forward slowly?",
      "a": "When you move slowly, nothing catches the ratchet on the reel, so the reel turns freely and the belt pays out. Only a sudden jerk or hard slowdown locks it.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a seatbelt reel work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What pulls the seatbelt back in?",
      "a": "A coiled spring inside the reel, like a clock spring, gently winds the belt back. It keeps the belt snug and rolls it away when you unbuckle.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does the seatbelt retract?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the pendulum in a seatbelt do?",
      "a": "The small pendulum weight keeps moving when the car slows suddenly, so it swings forward. Its swing tips up the pawl that locks the reel.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a seatbelt sense a crash?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the locking pawl?",
      "a": "The pawl is a small hooked lever. When it tips up it catches the teeth of the ratchet wheel on the reel and stops the belt unwinding.",
      "asks": [
        "What locks the seatbelt?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does my seatbelt lock if I pull it fast?",
      "a": "A second sensor in the reel has small weights that fly outward when the reel spins fast. They catch the ratchet teeth too, so a sharp tug locks the belt just as a crash would.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does the seatbelt get stuck when I yank it?",
        "What is the toothed ratchet in a seatbelt?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The three-point seatbelt was developed by Volvo engineer Nils Bohlin and introduced in 1959.",
    "Volvo let other carmakers use the three-point seatbelt design freely.",
    "Many cars also have pretensioners that pull the belt tight in the first moment of a crash."
  ],
